ll  that much as they were a pretty small  community but as cryptocurrency becomes  larger and the more people jump on board  it's only natural that the mining  community has also gotten quite large if  miners were simply buying gpus on a  regular basis this wouldn't really be a  problem  but the interest in mining is highly  correlated with the bitcoin market  cycles which have thus far been highly  seasonal for example let's take a look  at the 2017 bitcoin cycle and compare it  to nvidia's net income as we can see  ethereum which is what people were  predominantly mining with nvidia gpus  peaked in early 2018. some lasting  momentum along with the release of  nvidia's 2000 series of gpus carried the  company throughout much of 2018 but as  the hype surrounding the new gpu settled  down and miners flocked the scene  nvidia's net income got obliterated  within just two quarters nvidia's net  income crashed 68 percent from 1.23  billion dollars to 394 million dollars  it was until the last quarter of 2020  when bitcoin returned to its all-time  high the nvidia's net income also  recovered since then nvidia's income has  only gone up but as we saw in 2018 it  takes about a year for the cryptocycle  to reflect an nvidia's bottom line  also nvidia is supposed to release the  4000 series of gpus this year similar to  2000 series in 2018  so that should help hold up your income  for some time but as we get into the end  of this year and especially the  beginning of next year the weak crypto  market will likely weigh down on  nvidia's income quite substantially  even if crypto markets do recover within  the next 12 months it's going to be  difficult for nvidia to maintain their  current numbers because nvidia and  miners are slowly parting ways from  nvidia side they've been limiting the  performance of their gpus if they're  used for mining the goal with this limit  is to mitigate the demand from miners  and make more gpus available for  everyone else now of course hackers have  figured out a way to bypass these limits  but that's besides the point the point  is that nvidia is finally taking action  to limit the number of gpus that are  sold to miners  this was something that nvidia was  previously extremely reluctant to do for  obvious reasons however nvidia has been  switching their stands likely because  they realize that in the long term they  will lose the mind demographic no matter  what and when that happens they want to  ensure that they have a strong core of  gamers that are still loyal to their  brand but wait a minute why will nvidia  lose out on a mining demographic in the  first place well the answer boils down  to two reasons starting with asics  basics or application specific  integrated circuits are hardware that  are specifically built for a given  computational purpose which in our case  is mining in the early days mining asics  weren't that prevalent given that there  wasn't much demand for them but nowadays  flaunt companies like bitmain have been  built on just selling mining asics and  given that mining asics are specifically  built for mining they're way better than  nvidia's gaming gpus for mining now  nvidia has launched their own mining  specific hardware so they might make a  targeted move in the crypto market but  the days of using gaming gpus for mining  are definitely coming to an end  something else to consider is that much  of the crypto market is trying to move  away from mining altogether due to how  wasteful the process is  after all in mind you have to buy loads  of expensive hardware and constantly  burn mounds of electricity to keep them  running so cryptos like ethereum are  trying to switch to a proof of stake  model from a proof of work model both of  these models are rather complex to  explain but all you need to know is that  the new method does not require super  powerful hardware the founder of  ethereum vitalik buterin has already  been trying to make the switch for years  and he may be trying for years to come  as well  but at this point it seems like a  transition to proof-of-stake not just  for ethereum but cryptocurrencies as a  whole is inevitable and as crypto and  nvidia severed their ties there's no  question that nvidia's financials will  take a hit now it might seem like i'm  overstating nvidia's dependence on  mining but this is not the case in fact  the sec justified nvidia for not  disclosing how dependent they were on  crypto mining to investors considering  this declining demand from miners is by  far the biggest short-term threat for  nvidia  another major threat for nvidia's  business is through unsustainable price  increases over the past several years  nvidia has enjoyed extremely strong  pricing power due to overwhelming demand  for one miners are willing to pay way  more for nvidia's gpus than gamers  because it's an investment for them not  just entertainment and secondly the  supply shortage and more specifically  the chip shortage has made it impossible  for nvidia to keep up with the demand  this deadly combination has led to gpu  selling for unreal amounts on secondary  markets take the rtx 3090 for example  the rtx 3090 launched with a sticker  price of fifteen hundred dollars this  itself is super expensive until you take  a look at how much his gpus are selling  for all on secondary markets  this listing for example comes in at an  eye watering of four thousand eight  hundred dollars or over three times the  launch price now given that this is the  resale market nvidia doesn't actually  make anything from these ludicrous  resale prices however this has allowed  nvidia to substantially raise the launch  prices of their new gpus for example in  2017 nvidia's flagship gpu the gtx 1080  ti launched for 700  last year nvidia launched the 3000  series equivalent the 3080ti for 1200  this means that nvidia was able to raise  prices by 71 within a matter of four  years and that that's not even  considering their new additions 